I think when you ignore the market  dynamics and ignore what the market can  tell you about these things you do so at  your peril and you're inviting  unanticipated consequences that could be  worse than the problem you're trying to  solve hi i'm dan hayes with reason TV  today we are joined by author rob cellco  it's the author most recently of young  world rising how youth technology and  entrepreneurship are changing the world  from the bottom up rob is also a  business consultant and contributor to  the website Fast Company Rob thanks for  joining us thanks great to be here can  you define the term young world and give  us an overview sort of a broad argument  of what your book is about sure of the  6.7 billion people on earth right now  more than half of them are under the age  of 30 and the largest number of those  live in underdeveloped countries for the  most part or emerging economies like  India and China as we move toward  mid-century the difference between the  aging developed world and the young  developing world is going to accentuate  and I see a lot of possibilities for  discovery of talent in this young world  and so these folks who have grown up  with this technology that's part of the  argument as folks have grown up with  this technology they're used to it and  they're using it in their everyday lives  as opposed to that older generation  who's not sure and we've seen that here  in the US and in other industrialized  countries over the last 25 years because  you know we're at the front edge of this  stuff so we're the where the fraud  that's been boiling in the tank  elsewhere in the world a lot of these  technologies are not only relatively  newly arrived but they're arrived at a  much farther state of development and  younger people are taking to social  networks to mobile communication to  these new technologies that have been so  disruptive here in the US in really  interesting ways in these younger parts  of the world and as technology access  increases driven partly by market forces  I think we're going to see that dynamic  accelerate you start the book with a  story of a young guy named Sue Haas  gopinath in Bangalore India what is his  story and how is it sort of  representative of your argument in this  book so sue house is great he was a 14  years old young kid in  from humble background and he started  working after school at a cyber cafe  taught himself to do web development and  started a business for himself creating  websites for businesses in the u.s.  particularly small manufacturers that  had an email address but no website so  he would email these folks and say I'm  mr. gopinath from India I'd like to  order a 10,000 of your widgets please  direct me to your website and they would  say we don't have a website but we have  a catalog and you'd say I'm sorry you  don't meet my supply standards by the  way would you like to buy a website and  uh you know it's it sounds a little  shady but he is now studied in Indian  marketing programs for the ingenuity of  this plan he's grown his company into a  global company he's in 12 different  countries it's a multi-million dollar  concern and he is the youngest member of  the World Economic Forum so he's sipping  champagne in Davos with Bill Clinton and  Warren Buffett but he started as a 14  year old and a cybercafe basically and  he's only 22 now and he's and he's  looking for the next thing to do because  you know he's done with that and that's  incredible so you said you'd like to  look particularly at the unique  relationship between youth technology  adoption and the willingness to take  entrepreneurial risk so what what is the  relationship there well there's a couple  of things one thing is that the spread  of Technology and particularly high tech  business models or much less  capital-intensive so you can start a  business the way sue Haas did with  almost no investment except your own  expertise because you've already got the  platform to reach a global market to tap  a global talent base so that sort of  changes the economics a little bit makes  the entry costs a lot lower and also the  rewards are higher because for the  people that have the talent to do this  especially in developing countries it's  like you know you don't have to work in  the t shirt factory you don't have to  work in the in the mind you know if you  can figure this out you can earn a  decent living for yourself in you know  dignified working conditions and give  employment to other people and sort of  move society forward in you know better  in different ways and that's if you have  accessed the technology that allows that  to happen absolutely not just the  technology but also the skills and  know-how in the book you suggest that  this young world is more adept at  figuring out market-based solutions to  social problem  so can you kind of talk about this idea  of social entrepreneurship and how  that's how that's addressed in the book  sure it's kind of an interesting concept  because here in the US and in industrial  economies we have these concepts of  social entrepreneurship which is you  know grant funded nonprofit that's  trying to do good in the world and then  there's corporate social responsibility  which is a commercial business that's  putting their own you know out of the  goodness of their heart trying to solve  a solve a problem elsewhere in the world  the the idea of commercial prosperity  can't be separated from social progress  and let me give you an example like in  India Infosys the enormously successful  IT services company there spends a huge  amount of money training young people  even graduates of good schools to get  them up to this level of global  employability that they would have that  they need to be effectively competitive  but they don't stop adjust the elite  institutions they actually deliberately  broaden their search because they know  that there's a social impact of picking  some kid out of a small rural college  you know as one of the the chosen few  that's going to get to work in Infosys  goes back home becomes an ambassador for  knowledge economy becomes an ambassador  for entrepreneurship and the VP of  Infosys said to me if we stop doing that  if we narrow that search for talent and  and cut off that access to opportunity  the whole India story is over and she  said the India story not the Infosys  story and you know because Infosys sees  its role in the indian economy in  broader terms than just its own  prosperity and it recognizes that if it  can't deploy its own resources to make  progress on these issues its commercial  success as a private business is in  peril and you seem optimistic about  technology in general and technology in  the future what's your grand sort of  utopian vision going for it and 20 years  what are we going to be doing that we  can't do now well i would say technology  utopianism isn't my orientation I'm  actually rather pessimistic about the  general direction of things I look at  technology is the only way we're going  to solve these problems because there's  a real deficit of public will to address  common challenges right now so our best  ho  is to by broadening a conversation out  to a billion people makes it more likely  that we're going to find that one in a  billion idea because that's that's the  one we need so talk a little bit about  your politics you've kind of argued that  there's a top-down hierarchical sort of  archaic system in place and that this  young world is going to provide a  bottom-up you know way of exchanging  information can you kind of talk about  that sounds a little libertarian can you  kind of talk about your personal  politics and the politics of this book  yeah i mean i think that large  organizations serve the 20th century  pretty well and because centralized  hierarchies or a good way of organizing  large organizations to get stuff done  move into the 21st century in these  networks start to offer us other options  about how to manage organizations  organizations that can be a little bit  more nimble that can be a little bit  more responsive to the local needs and  local cultures and local desires of the  people that are doing them so you're not  forcing people a choice between you know  you either take the big solution or no  solution you know we can actually  customize and so I think that is not  only you know a good way to run a  business is a good way to solve social  problems and I do feel deeply that there  are these social problems we have a  common responsibility to fix them but we  need to look at market-based mechanisms  for making those solutions happen in  ways that are relevant to real people so  you mentioned before you're more of a  progressive guy but you look for these  market-based solutions to solve the  problems I think when you ignore the  market dynamics and ignore what the  market can tell you about these things  you do so at your peril and you're  inviting unanticipated consequences that  could be worse than the problem you're  trying to solve at the same time if you  only pay attention to the mechanism and  not to the larger issue of what is it  we're trying to do what outcomes we're  trying to produce you know you create a  very efficient road to nowhere so we  have to we have to get beyond that  dichotomy because our problems are too  big for that kind of thinking so lastly  why should somebody read this book if  you're a young entrepreneur you're going  to find some really great ideas some  really inspiring stories of people that  are making great solutions out of almost  nothing  if you're a business leader you really  need to understand what's going on in  these parts of the world emerging  markets are where the growth are or  where the growth is and there's a lot of  opportunities to engage with these young  world partners and to understand the  sources of young world competition  before it arrives in our shores that's  great well thanks so much for joining us  Rob I enjoyed talking with you if you  want to learn more about Rob's book  visit young world rising calm and for  recent TV i'm dan hayes

